"""One reusable graph example, followed by the connection to OSPF routing."""

from . import lesson, orbit, planet

DIJKSTRA = planet(
    "dijkstra", "Dijkstra & OSPF", "#7cc4ff",
    "You are planning routes between space stations. The fewest jumps may not be the cheapest "
    "journey: each link has a cost. Learn to model the map, improve candidate distances, and "
    "prove when a route is final. Then connect that local shortest-path computation to the "
    "topology advertisements used by OSPF routers. All graph examples use nonnegative costs "
    "unless a lesson explicitly demonstrates a failure case.",
    [
        orbit(1, "Model the route map", [
            lesson("graphs-and-paths", "Graphs and paths",
                "Vertices represent places and edges represent permitted movements between them.",
                "Translate a route problem into a directed, weighted graph.", [
                    ("Build the model", "Represent each station as a vertex and each permitted journey as an edge. An arrow A → B means you may travel from A to B; it does not automatically permit B → A. An undirected connection represents permission in both directions."),
                    ("Choose what a weight means", "A weight might measure time, fuel, or an administrative route cost. Choose one quantity and consistent units. A 4-minute link and a 1-unit fuel link cannot simply be added into a meaningful journey cost without a defined conversion or objective."),
                    ("Use our shared example", "The directed links are A→B:4, A→C:1, C→B:2, B→D:1, C→D:5, D→E:3, and C→E:10. There are no other links. We will reuse this exact graph, with A as the source, so you can check every later step."),
                    ("Distinguish a route from a shortcut", "A path must follow existing arrows. A→C→B is legal and costs 1+2=3. A→D is not a direct edge, even if two stations appear close in a drawing. Screen positions are presentation; edges and weights define the mathematical problem."),
                    ("Explain the input", "List the outgoing neighbours of C: B, D, and E. Ask whether the network is static during one calculation and whether a destination is reachable. [[path-costs]] explains why visiting fewer edges is not necessarily cheaper."),
                ], "In our directed graph, which journey is explicitly permitted?",
                ["E→C because C→E exists", "A→C→B using the listed arrows", "A→D as a direct link"],
                "A→C→B using the listed arrows", ["When should a graph be directed?", "Why are screen positions not edge costs?"]),
            lesson("path-costs", "Path costs and the objective",
                "Shortest means minimum total edge weight, which can differ from minimum hop count.",
                "Compare alternative paths and choose BFS or Dijkstra for the stated objective.", [
                    ("Add along the journey", "For a path, add its edge weights. A→B costs 4. A→C→B costs 1+2=3, so the two-edge journey is cheaper. Dijkstra's 'shortest' refers to the chosen total weight, not geometric length or the number of station names in the route."),
                    ("Compare another destination", "To reach D, A→B→D costs 4+1=5. A→C→D costs 1+5=6. A→C→B→D costs 1+2+1=4. The best route here uses more hops than either obvious two-hop alternative."),
                    ("Know when BFS is enough", "If every edge has the same positive cost, minimising total cost is equivalent to minimising hops, and breadth-first search is suitable. With differing nonnegative weights, Dijkstra explores by total known cost rather than by hop layers."),
                    ("Define what is not being solved", "A shortest-path calculation does not automatically minimise congestion, maximise reliability, or visit every station. If costs change while you travel, the original answer may become outdated. State the objective and the snapshot being solved before calling a path optimal."),
                    ("Predict the answer", "Using the graph from [[graphs-and-paths]], try to find the cheapest route from A to E. The result will be 7 via C, B, and D. We will derive it mechanically after learning [[tentative-distances]] and [[edge-relaxation]], rather than relying on visual guessing."),
                ], "A direct link costs 4 while a two-link route costs 1 then 2. Which has lower total cost?",
                ["The two-link route with cost 3", "The direct link because it uses fewer hops", "They must tie"],
                "The two-link route with cost 3", ["When can BFS replace Dijkstra?", "What makes a path optimal?"]),
            lesson("tentative-distances", "Tentative distances",
                "Distance estimates describe the best routes found so far, not necessarily final answers.",
                "Initialise distances and distinguish unknown, tentative, and settled vertices.", [
                    ("Start with one known route", "Set dist[A]=0 because travelling from A to itself requires no edges. Initialise every other distance to infinity, meaning no route has been found yet. Infinity is a sentinel for unknown reachability, not a giant real edge in the graph."),
                    ("Discover the neighbours", "After examining A's outgoing edges, set dist[B]=4 and dist[C]=1. D and E remain infinity. These are the best routes discovered so far; B's value is not yet final because a cheaper route through C may still exist."),
                    ("Track the predecessor", "Alongside a distance, remember the predecessor that produced it. Initially parent[B]=A and parent[C]=A. When a cheaper route is found, update both the distance and predecessor. That information later reconstructs an actual path, not only its price."),
                    ("Use precise states", "A discovered vertex has a finite candidate distance. A settled vertex has been selected as the smallest current candidate and is final under Dijkstra's assumptions. Discovering, adding to a queue, and settling are different events. Mixing them can cause a premature answer."),
                    ("Keep unreachable vertices honest", "If no route from A reaches a vertex, its distance stays infinity when the search finishes. Do not invent a predecessor or report a zero-length route to it. Next, [[edge-relaxation]] shows the operation that improves tentative values."),
                ], "After exploring only A, dist[B]=4. What does this mean?",
                ["B is definitely unreachable", "Every route to B costs exactly 4", "The cheapest route found so far costs 4"],
                "The cheapest route found so far costs 4", ["Why initialise the source to zero?", "When is a distance final rather than tentative?"]),
            lesson("edge-relaxation", "Edge relaxation",
                "Relaxing an edge tests whether a known route through its source improves its destination.",
                "Calculate candidate distances and update predecessors only on improvement.", [
                    ("Write the rule", "For an edge u→v with cost w, compute candidate = dist[u] + w. If candidate < dist[v], replace dist[v] and set parent[v]=u. Relaxation means loosening an overly large upper estimate by supplying a better actual route."),
                    ("Work through C to B", "After A, the candidates are C:1 and B:4. Exploring C→B:2 produces 1+2=3. Since 3<4, update B to 3 and replace its parent A with C. You have discovered a real improvement rather than merely encountering B again."),
                    ("Try a non-improvement", "Once B has distance 3, B→D:1 proposes D:4. If D previously had 6 via C, update it. An alternative proposal of 8 would be ignored. Distances decrease as routes improve; do not overwrite a better route with the newest route seen."),
                    ("Handle equal routes deliberately", "If candidate equals dist[v], the cheapest price is unchanged. A basic path reconstruction can keep one parent. A routing application interested in equal-cost alternatives needs additional bookkeeping rather than blindly replacing or duplicating a single parent."),
                    ("Relate local work to the search", "Relaxation alone is not the whole algorithm: many shortest-path methods use it. Dijkstra's defining choice is which vertex to explore next and when that choice is safe. [[greedy-settlement]] explains why the smallest tentative distance can become permanent with nonnegative edges."),
                ], "dist[C]=1, dist[B]=4, and C→B costs 2. What does relaxation do?",
                ["Set dist[B]=2", "Set dist[B]=3 and parent[B]=C", "Keep dist[B]=4 because B was already discovered"],
                "Set dist[B]=3 and parent[B]=C", ["Why must the predecessor change with an improved distance?", "What should happen when two candidate costs are equal?"]),
        ]),
        orbit(2, "Run and explain Dijkstra", [
            lesson("greedy-settlement", "Greedy settlement",
                "The smallest unsettled tentative distance can be finalised when all edge costs are nonnegative.",
                "Explain the safe greedy choice and avoid stopping on first discovery.", [
                    ("Choose by total distance", "Among discovered unsettled vertices, select the one with the smallest tentative distance. In our example C:1 is selected before B:4. This is not choosing the smallest outgoing edge from your current station; it is comparing complete candidate distances from A."),
                    ("Understand why it is safe", "Suppose a cheaper route to the selected vertex existed. Before reaching it, that route would cross from settled to unsettled territory. Relaxing that crossing would have supplied an unsettled candidate no more costly than the alleged better route. Nonnegative remaining edges prevent a late discount."),
                    ("Use the result", "The smallest candidate can therefore be settled: its shortest distance from A is final. Relax its outgoing edges and repeat. Equal smallest distances can be handled in either order while preserving shortest costs, though the particular predecessor tree may differ."),
                    ("Do not stop too soon", "If a target is first discovered at cost 10, a later route may cost 7. For a single destination, stop when that target is removed as the current valid minimum and settled, not when it is first inserted into the queue."),
                    ("Challenge the assumption", "The argument relies on nonnegative edges. A negative edge can make a previously expensive route suddenly cheaper after a vertex was settled. We build a counterexample at [[nonnegative-weights]]. First, [[priority-queue]] makes selecting the minimum efficient."),
                ], "When is it safe to stop a standard Dijkstra search for one target?",
                ["When the target is settled as the valid minimum candidate", "When its name first appears in the queue", "After examining any edge pointing towards it"],
                "When the target is settled as the valid minimum candidate", ["Is Dijkstra choosing the cheapest edge or the cheapest known route?", "Why do nonnegative weights matter to the proof?"]),
            lesson("priority-queue", "Priority queue and stale entries",
                "A minimum-priority queue selects the cheapest candidate without scanning all vertices each time.",
                "Use a heap correctly when an improved distance leaves an old entry behind.", [
                    ("Store a useful pair", "A minimum-priority queue can hold pairs (distance, vertex). Begin with (0,A), remove the minimum, and push new candidates after successful relaxations. The data structure's priority is the current route cost, not the alphabetic vertex name."),
                    ("See the duplicate problem", "Exploring A pushes (4,B) and (1,C). Exploring C improves B to 3 and pushes (3,B). If the heap implementation does not support decrease-key, (4,B) can remain in the heap beside the better entry."),
                    ("Discard stale work", "When removing (cost,u), compare cost with the current dist[u]. If they differ, that entry is obsolete and should be skipped. The old (4,B) must not settle B again or overwrite the already improved distance of 3."),
                    ("Run the loop in Python", "This complete teaching version uses string vertex labels and finite nonnegative weights. A vertex with no outgoing edges may be omitted from the dictionary. It returns distances and predecessors.\n\n```python\nfrom heapq import heappop, heappush\n\ndef dijkstra(graph, source):\n    vertices = set(graph) | {v for edges in graph.values() for v, _ in edges}\n    vertices.add(source)\n    if any(weight < 0 for edges in graph.values() for _, weight in edges):\n        raise ValueError('Dijkstra requires nonnegative weights')\n    distances = {v: float('inf') for v in vertices}\n    parents = {}\n    distances[source] = 0\n    heap = [(0, source)]\n    while heap:\n        cost, u = heappop(heap)\n        if cost != distances[u]:\n            continue\n        for v, weight in graph.get(u, []):\n            candidate = cost + weight\n            if candidate < distances[v]:\n                distances[v] = candidate\n                parents[v] = u\n                heappush(heap, (candidate, v))\n    return distances, parents\n```"),
                    ("Compare implementation choices", "An indexed heap can update an existing priority with decrease-key. A simple heap often uses duplicates and stale-entry checks instead. Both can be correct; their memory and detailed complexity differ. Follow the complete run at [[worked-shortest-path]] before optimising the queue."),
                ], "The heap returns (4,B), but the distance table now says dist[B]=3. What should you do?",
                ["Reset B to 4", "Restart the entire search", "Skip the stale entry"],
                "Skip the stale entry", ["Why can duplicate heap entries be correct?", "How does decrease-key differ from pushing a replacement?"]),
            lesson("worked-shortest-path", "A complete shortest-path trace",
                "Run the same weighted map to completion and inspect every improvement.",
                "Reproduce the settled order and final distance table for a small directed graph.", [
                    ("Settle A", "Start A:0 with all other distances infinite. Settle A and relax its edges, giving B:4 and C:1. The best candidate is C. Keep a table with columns vertex, tentative distance, predecessor, and whether it is settled."),
                    ("Settle C", "C has distance 1. Relax C→B:2, improving B from 4 to 3; C→D:5 gives D:6; C→E:10 gives E:11. The frontier now contains useful candidates B:3, D:6, E:11. The old heap entry B:4 is stale."),
                    ("Settle B, then D", "B:3 is next. Its edge B→D:1 improves D to 4, replacing parent[D]=C with B. Settle D:4 and relax D→E:3, improving E from 11 to 7. Ignore any obsolete queue entries when they appear."),
                    ("Read the result", "E:7 is the final useful candidate. The settled order is A, C, B, D, E; final distances are A:0, B:3, C:1, D:4, E:7. The route to E is A→C→B→D→E, whose weights sum to 1+2+1+3=7."),
                    ("Check it independently", "Compare A→C→E=11 and A→B→D→E=8. They are legal but more expensive. A drawing with a visually shorter route would not change these weights. [[reconstructing-paths]] shows how predecessors recover the winning route without storing a whole path in every queue item."),
                ], "What is the cheapest cost from A to E in the worked graph?",
                ["11", "7", "8"], "7",
                ["Which relaxation first improves D to 4?", "Why are B:4 and D:6 obsolete heap entries?"]),
            lesson("reconstructing-paths", "Reconstructing a path",
                "Predecessors turn the cheapest distance into an ordered route.",
                "Recover a path backwards and handle the source and unreachable destinations.", [
                    ("Keep the successful predecessor", "Whenever a strictly cheaper route to v is found through u, record parent[v]=u. In our final table parent[C]=A, parent[B]=C, parent[D]=B, and parent[E]=D. These are witnesses to the routes that produced the final distances."),
                    ("Walk backwards", "To recover A's route to E, start at E: predecessor D, then B, then C, then A. This gives E,D,B,C,A in reverse. Reverse that list to obtain A,C,B,D,E. The sum of its edge weights must equal dist[E]=7."),
                    ("Handle special cases", "For source A itself, return [A] with cost 0. If a destination's distance is infinity, report that no route was found; do not follow an undefined parent or return the destination alone as if it were reachable."),
                    ("Know what one tree represents", "Different equal-cost routes can give different valid predecessor trees. A single parent per vertex selects one route, not every possible shortest path. With zero-cost edges, careless equal-distance parent rewrites can even create confusing cycles; update only for strict improvements in the basic version."),
                    ("Use the output in routing", "A router typically needs the next hop, not a full path attached to each packet. The first neighbour after A in the reconstructed route to E is C. [[ospf-link-state]] connects this source-rooted view to a routing table built independently at each router."),
                ], "Following E's predecessors gives E,D,B,C,A. What is the forward route?",
                ["A,C,B,D,E", "A,B,C,D,E", "E,D,B,C,A"], "A,C,B,D,E",
                ["Why might two correct implementations return different equal-cost routes?", "How should an unreachable destination be represented?"]),
        ]),
        orbit(3, "Limits and real networks", [
            lesson("nonnegative-weights", "Why weights must be nonnegative",
                "Negative edges can invalidate the claim that a settled distance can never improve.",
                "Construct a counterexample and identify an appropriate alternative algorithm.", [
                    ("Build a small counterexample", "Consider directed edges A→B:2, A→C:5, and C→B:-4. Starting at A, a standard settled-once Dijkstra implementation settles B at 2 before C at 5. Yet A→C→B costs 1, so the early finalisation was wrong."),
                    ("Find the broken reasoning", "The unseen route through C initially looks more expensive, but the negative edge later discounts it. Nonnegative edges ruled out precisely that possibility in [[greedy-settlement]]. The failure is about the proof, not simply a limitation of a particular heap library."),
                    ("Do not confuse zero with negative", "Zero-weight edges are allowed by Dijkstra's nonnegative assumption. They can create ties and multiple shortest routes, but they do not provide a negative discount. Ensure the implementation still handles equal distances and queue entries consistently."),
                    ("Recognise an even harder case", "A reachable negative cycle can reduce path cost again and again, so some destinations have no finite minimum. Bellman-Ford can handle negative edges and detect relevant negative cycles. A directed acyclic graph permits another approach based on topological ordering."),
                    ("Choose rather than patch blindly", "Adding the same constant to every edge is not a general fix: routes with different numbers of edges receive different extra costs, changing the winner. Explain why validating the graph's assumptions should happen before promising a shortest-path answer."),
                ], "Why is adding the same positive constant to every edge not a general repair for negative weights?",
                ["It always creates a cycle", "Routes with different hop counts gain different total extra costs", "It removes all edges"],
                "Routes with different hop counts gain different total extra costs", ["Are zero-weight edges safe?", "What does a negative cycle mean for a shortest-path answer?"]),
            lesson("dijkstra-complexity", "Time, memory, and graph representation",
                "The queue and graph representation determine the implementation's running time.",
                "Interpret the standard complexity bounds and avoid confusing them with hop counts.", [
                    ("Count the input", "Let V be the number of vertices and E the number of directed edges. An adjacency list stores each vertex's outgoing neighbours and uses O(V+E) space. An adjacency matrix uses O(V²) cells even when only a few links exist."),
                    ("Separate the work", "The search scans outgoing edges to relax them and repeatedly selects the next minimum candidate. A simple implementation that scans all vertices for each minimum takes O(V²+E). This can be reasonable for dense graphs or small teaching examples."),
                    ("Use a heap deliberately", "With an adjacency list and a binary heap supporting decrease-key, the usual bound is O((V+E) log V). A lazy heap can hold O(E+1) candidates; including initialisation, a safe bound is O(V + (E+1) log(E+2)). For simple graphs this is commonly expressed as O((V+E) log V), apart from trivial tiny cases."),
                    ("Account for memory", "Distance and predecessor tables use O(V). The graph uses O(V+E). An indexed heap can stay O(V), while a duplicate-entry heap may use O(E). These costs describe stored work, not the number of stations on the one final path."),
                    ("Ask what actually matters", "A heap is not automatically faster for every tiny or dense instance. Model size, representation, constants, and update frequency all matter. OSPF adds topology exchange and convergence work around the local shortest-path calculation; [[ospf-link-state]] introduces that distinction."),
                ], "With adjacency lists and a binary heap with decrease-key, what is the standard Dijkstra time bound?",
                ["O(1)", "O(V!)", "O((V+E) log V)"], "O((V+E) log V)",
                ["Why can lazy heap duplicates use more memory?", "When might an O(V²) implementation be reasonable?"]),
            lesson("ospf-link-state", "OSPF and the shared topology map",
                "OSPF exchanges link-state information so routers can compute source-rooted routes locally.",
                "Distinguish topology dissemination from Dijkstra's local shortest-path computation.", [
                    ("Give each router a map", "OSPF is a link-state routing protocol used within an autonomous system. Routers exchange advertisements describing topology and costs. In an area, participating routers aim to maintain a consistent link-state database, rather than merely trusting a neighbour's final route recommendation."),
                    ("Run the algorithm locally", "Each router uses itself as the root of a shortest-path calculation over the relevant topology. Two routers can share the same map yet build different source-rooted shortest-path trees. The resulting routing tables identify destinations, costs, and next hops from their own locations."),
                    ("Relate it to the station map", "If router A uses our teaching graph, its route to E begins with C. Router D would use D as its source and may have a different view of reachability in that directed example. A topology database is shared input; a routing table is a local derived result."),
                    ("Respect the protocol details", "OSPF models routers and networks and has area and route-type rules beyond this small graph. Costs are configured routing metrics, not necessarily measured latency. Computing shortest paths is a central ingredient, but OSPF is more than calling a generic Dijkstra function."),
                    ("Explain the division of labour", "Advertisements tell routers what the map currently is; the shortest-path algorithm tells each router which routes follow from that map. If a link fails, both information exchange and recalculation are needed. Follow that sequence at [[ospf-convergence]]."),
                ], "Why can two routers with the same topology database have different shortest-path trees?",
                ["Each uses itself as the source", "The algorithm must pick random costs", "One must have an incorrect database"],
                "Each uses itself as the source", ["How is a link-state database different from a routing table?", "Why is an OSPF cost not necessarily a delay measurement?"]),
            lesson("ospf-convergence", "Failures, convergence, and equal-cost routes",
                "A topology change must be detected, advertised, and incorporated before routing settles again.",
                "Trace a link failure and explain transient disagreement and equal-cost multipath.", [
                    ("Remove a real link", "Suppose the teaching link B→D fails. The old best path A→C→B→D→E is no longer usable. On the changed map, A→C→D→E costs 1+5+3=9, beating the alternative A→C→E=11. A previously optimal route is not permanently optimal."),
                    ("Propagate the new information", "A routing protocol first detects a changed adjacency or link state, then distributes updated information. Routers update their databases and recompute affected routes. Until this process converges, they may temporarily make decisions from different topology snapshots."),
                    ("Understand transient problems", "Different update timing can temporarily cause unreachable routes or forwarding loops. Dijkstra's correctness on a fixed input does not promise every router instantly shares that input. Protocol timers, flooding, and operational design affect how the network reaches a new stable state."),
                    ("Allow equal-cost alternatives", "If two valid routes have the same preferred metric, OSPF can support equal-cost multipath. A routing table may retain more than one next hop instead of forcing one arbitrary predecessor. That does not mean packets should be sent around every possible route without policy."),
                    ("Finish the route story", "You can now model a graph, relax edges, justify settlement, reconstruct a route, state complexity and assumptions, and explain how routers obtain their maps. Say which part fails if an edge is negative and which part becomes outdated if the physical network changes."),
                ], "After B→D fails in the teaching graph, what is the cheapest remaining A-to-E route cost?",
                ["7 because shortest paths never change", "9 via A→C→D→E", "11 via A→C→E"],
                "9 via A→C→D→E", ["Why can correct routers temporarily disagree after a failure?", "What extra bookkeeping does equal-cost multipath require?"]),
        ]),
    ],
)
